ze);\nL271:     std::vector<void *> dsts(batch_size);\nL272:     std::vector<size_t> sizes(batch_size);\nL273: \nL274:     // Allocate the source and destination buffers\nL275:     // initialize with the stream number\nL276:     for (size_t i = 0; i < batch_size; i++) {\nL277:         cudaMallocHost(&srcs[i], sizes[i]);\nL278:         cudaMalloc(&dsts[i], sizes[i]);\nL279:         cudaMemsetAsync(srcs[i], sizes[i], stream);\nL280:     }\nL281: \nL282:     // Setup attributes for this batch of copies\nL283:     cudaMemcpyAttributes attrs = {};\nL284:     attrs.srcAccessOrder = cudaMemcpySrcAccessOrderStream;\nL285: \nL286:     // All copies in the batch have same copy attributes.\nL287:     size_t attrsIdxs = 0;  // Index of the attributes\nL288: \nL289:     // Launch the batched memory transfer\nL290:     cudaMemcpyBatchAsync(&dsts[0], &srcs[0], &sizes[0], batch_size,\nL291:         &attrs, &attrsIdxs, 1 /*numAttrs*/, nullptr /*failIdx*/, stream);\nL292: The first few parameters to the `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync()` function seem immediately sensible. The are comprised of arrays containing the source and destination pointers, as well as the transfer sizes. Each array has to have``batch_size`` elements. The new information comes from the attributes. The function needs a pointer to an array of attributes, and a corresponding array of attribute indices.\nL293: In principle it is also possible to pass an array of `size_t` and in this array the indices of an failed transfers can be recorded, however it is safe to pass a `nullptr` here, in this case the indices of failures will simply not be recorded.\nL294: Turning to the attributes, in this instance the transfers are homogeneous. So we use only one attribute, which will apply to all the transfers. This is controlled by the attrIndex parameter. In principle this can be an array. Element i of the array contains the index of the first transfer to which the i-th element of the attribute array applies.\nL295: In this case, attrIndex is treated as a single element array, with the value ‘0’ meaning that `attribute[0]` will apply to all transfers with index 0 and up, in other words all the transfers.\nL296: Finally, we note that we have set the `srcAccessOrder` attribute to `cudaMemcpySrcAccessOrderStream`. This means that the source data will be accessed in regular stream order. In other words, the memcpy will block until previous kernels dealing with the data from any of these source and destination pointers are completed.\nL297: \nL298: In the next example we will consider a more complex case of a heterogeneous batch transfer.\nL299: Listing 5 Example of Heterogeneous Batched Memory Transfer using some Ephemeral Host Memory to Pinned Device Memorycite77†# L300: \nL301:     std::vector<void *> srcs(batch_size);\nL302:     std::vector<void *> dsts(batch_size);\nL303:     std::vector<size_t> sizes(batch_size);\nL304: \nL305:     // Allocate the src and dst buffers\nL306:     for (size_t i = 0; i < batch_size - 10; i++) {\nL307:         cudaMallocHost(&srcs[i], sizes[i]);\nL308:         cudaMalloc(&dsts[i], sizes[i]);\nL309:     }\nL310: \nL311:     int buffer[10];\nL312:     for (size_t i = batch_size - 10; i < batch_size; i++) {\nL313:         srcs[i] = &buffer[10 - (batch_size - i];\nL314:         cudaMalloc(&dsts[i], sizes[i]);\nL315:     }\nL316: \nL317:     // Setup attributes for this batch of copies\nL318:     cudaMemcpyAttributes attrs[2] = {};\nL319:     attrs[0].srcAccessOrder = cudaMemcpySrcAccessOrderStream;\nL320:     attrs[1].srcAccessOrder = cudaMemcpySrcAccessOrderDuringApiCall;\nL321: \nL322:     size_t attrsIdxs[2];\nL323:     attrsIdxs[0] = 0;\nL324:     attrsIdxs[1] = batch_size - 10;\nL325:     // Launch the batched memory transfer\nL326:     cudaMemcpyBatchAsync(&dsts[0], &srcs[0], &sizes[0], batch_size,\nL327:         &attrs, &attrsIdxs, 2 /*numAttrs*/, nullptr /*failIdx*/, stream);\nL328: Here we have two kinds of transfers: `batch_size-10` transfer from pinned host memory to pinned device memory, and 10 transfers from a host array to pinned device memory. Further, the buffer array is not only on the host but is only in existence in the current scope – its address is what is known as an ephemeral pointer. This pointer may not be valid after the API call completes (it is asynchronous).\nL329: To perform the copies with such ephemeral pointers, the srcAccessOrder in the attribute must be set to cudaMemcpySrcAccessOrderDuringApiCall.\nL330: We now have two attributes, the first one applies to all transfers with indices starting at 0, and less than `batch_size-10`. The second one applies to all transfers with indices starting at `batch_size-10` and less than `batch_size`.\nL331: If instead of allocating the buffer array from the stack, we had allocated it from the heap using malloc the data would not be ephemeral any more. It would be valid until the pointer was explicitly freed.\nL332: In such a case the best option for how to stage the copies would depend on whether the system had hardware managed memory or coherent GPU access to host memory via address translation in which case it would be best to use stream ordering, or whether it did not in which case staging the transfers immediately would make most sense. In this situation, one should use the value `cudaMemcpyAccessOrderAny` for the `srcAccessOrder` of the attribute.\nL333: The `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` function also allows the programmer to provide hints about the source and destination locations. This is done by setting the `srcLocation` and `dstLocation` fields of the `cudaMemcpyAttributes` structure. The `srcLocation``and ``dstLocation` fields are both of type `cudaMemLocation` which is a structure that contains the type of the location and the ID of the location.\nL334: This is the same `cudaMemLocation` structure that can be used to give prefetching hints to the runtime when using `cudaMemPrefetchAsync()`. We illustrate how to set up the hints for a transfer from the device, to a specific NUMA node of the host in the code example below:\nL335: Listing 6 Example of Setting Source and Destination Location Hintscite78†# L336: \nL337:     // Allocate the source and destination buffers\nL338:     std::vector<void *> srcs(batch_size);\nL339:     std::vector<void *> dsts(batch_size);\nL340:     std::vector<size_t> sizes(batch_size);\nL341: \nL342:     // cudaMemLocation structures we will use tp provide location hints\nL343:     // Device device_id\nL344:     cudaMemLocation srcLoc = {cudaMemLocationTypeDevice, dev_id};\nL345: \nL346:     // Host with numa Node numa_id\nL347:     cudaMemLocation dstLoc = {cudaMemLocationTypeHostNuma, numa_id};\nL348: \nL349:     // Allocate the src and dst buffers\nL350:     for (size_t i = 0; i < batch_size; i++) {\nL351:         cudaMallocManaged(&srcs[i], sizes[i]);\nL352:         cudaMallocManaged(&dsts[i], sizes[i]);\nL353: \nL354:         cudaMemPrefetchAsync(srcs[i], sizes[i], srcLoc, 0, stream);\nL355:         cudaMemPrefetchAsync(dsts[i], sizes[i], dstLoc, 0, stream);\nL356:         cudaMemsetAsync(srcs[i], sizes[i], stream);\nL357:     }\nL358: \nL359:     // Setup attributes for this batch of copies\nL360:     cudaMemcpyAttributes attrs = {};\nL361: \nL362:     // These are managed memory pointers so Stream Order is appropriate\nL363:     attrs.srcAccessOrder = cudaMemcpySrcAccessOrderStream;\nL364: \nL365:     // Now we can specify the location hints here.\nL366:     attrs.srcLocHint = srcLoc;\nL367:     attrs.dstlocHint = dstLoc;\nL368: \nL369:     // All copies in the batch have same copy attributes.\nL370:     size_t attrsIdxs = 0;\nL371: \nL372:     // Launch the batched memory transfer\nL373:     cudaMemcpyBatchAsync(&dsts[0], &srcs[0], &sizes[0], batch_size,\nL374:         &attrs, &attrsIdxs, 1 /*numAttrs*/, nullptr /*failIdx*/, stream);\nL375: THe last thing to cover is the flag for hinting whether we want to use SM’s or CEs for the transfers. The field for this is the `cudaMemcpyAttributesflags::flags` and the possible values are:\nL376: \nL377:   * `cudaMemcpyFlagDefault` – default behavior\nL378: \nL379:   * `cudaMemcpyFlagPreferOverlapWithCompute` – this hints that the system should prefer to use CEs for the transfers overlapping the transfer with computations.\nL380: \nL381: In summary, the main points regarding “cudaMemcpyBatchAsync” are as follows:\nL382:   * The `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` function (and its 3D variant) allows the programmer to specify a batch of memory transfers, allowing the amortization of transfer setup overheads.\nL383:   * Other than the source and destination pointers and the transfer sizes, the function can take one or more memory copy attributes providing information about the kind of memory being transferred and the corresponding stream ordering behavior of the source pointers, hints about the source and destination locations, and hints as to whether to prefer to overlap the transfer with compute (if possible) or whether to use SMs for the transfer.\nL384:   * Given the above information the runtime can attempt to optimize the transfer to the maximum degree possible..\nL385: ## 3.1.6. Environment Variablescite79†# L386: \nL387: CUDA provides various environment variables (see cite80†Section 5.2 ), which can affect execution and performance. If they are not explicitly set, CUDA uses reasonable default values for them, but special handling may be required on a per-case basis, e.g., for debugging purposes or to get improved performance.\nL388: For example, increasing the value of the `CUDA_DEVICE_MAX_CONNECTIONS` environment variable may be necessary to reduce the possibility that independent work from different CUDA streams gets serialized due to false dependencies. Such false dependencies may be introduced when the same underlying resource(s) are used.\nL389: It is recommended to start by using the default value and only explore the impact of this environment variable in case of performance issues (e.g., unexpected serialization of independent work across CUDA streams that cannot be attributed to other factors like lack of available SM resources). Worth noting that this environment variable has a different (lower) default value in case of MPS.\nL390: Similarly, setting the `CUDA_MODULE_LOADING` environment variable to `EAGER` may be preferable for latency-sensitive applications, in order to move all overhead due to module loading to the application initialization phase and outside its critical phase. The current default mode is lazy module loading.\nL391: In this default mode, a similar effect to eager module loading could be achieved by adding “warm-up” calls of the various kernels during the application’s initialization phase, to force module loading to happen sooner.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2.5. Asynchronous Execution — CUDA Programming Guide (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/02-basics/asynchronous-execution.html)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/02-basics/asynchronous-execution.html\",\"pattern\":\"overlap\"}); Total lines: 564\nL123: In order for memory copies involving CPU memory to be carried out asynchronously, the host buffers must be pinned and page-locked. `cudaMemcpyAsync()` will function correctly if host memory which is not pinned and page-locked is used, but it will revert to a synchronous behavior which will not overlap with other work. This can inhibit the performance benefits of using asynchronous memory transfers.\nL260: We then go into a CPU work loop, but occasionally take a peek to see if the event has completed indicating that kernel1 is done. If so, we launch a device to host copy into stream stream2. This approach allows the overlap of the CPU work with the GPU kernel execution and the device to host copy.\nL419: There are some special cases where these semantics may be relaxed for purposes of performance optimization such as in the case of a cite76†programmatic dependent kernel launch , which allows the overlap of two kernels through the use of special attributes and kernel launch mechanisms, or in the case of batching memory transfers using the cite77†asynchronous batched memory copy functions , when the runtime can perform non-overlapping batch copies concurrently.\nL542: >   * Asynchronous APIs allow us to express concurrent execution of tasks providing the way to express overlapping of various operations. The actual concurrency achieved is dependent on available hardware resources and compute-capabilities.\nL543: >\nL544: >   * The key abstractions in CUDA for asynchronous execution are streams, events and callback functions.\nL545: >\nL546: >   * Synchronization is possible at the event, stream and device level\nL547: >\nL548: >   * The default stream is a blocking stream which synchronizes with all other blocking streams, but does not synchronize with non-blocking streams\nL549: >\nL550: >   * The default stream behavior can be avoided using per-thread default streams via the `--default-stream per-thread` compiler option or the CUDA_API_PER_THREAD_DEFAULT_STREAM preprocessor macro.\nL551: >\nL552: >   * Streams can be created with different priorities, which are hints to the runtime and may not be respected for memory transfers.\nL553: >\nL554: >   * CUDA provides API functions to reduce, or overlap overheads of kernel launches and memory transfers such as CUDA Graphs, Batched Memory Transfers and Programmatic Dependent Kernel Launch.\nL555: On this page\nL556: \nL557:   * cite78†2.5.6. Blocking and non-blocking streams and the default stream L558: \nL559: cite91†Privacy Policy†www.nvidia.com | cite92†Your Privacy Choices†www.nvidia.com | cite93†Terms of Service†www.nvidia.com | cite94†Accessibility†www.nvidia.com | cite95†Corporate Policies†www.nvidia.com | cite96†Product Security†